{"name":"Lemon Curry","hex":"#cda323","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lemon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A yellowish citrus fruit.","A semitropical evergreen tree, Citrus limon, that bears such fruits."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing or having the flavour/flavor and/or scent of lemons.","Of the pale yellow colour/color of lemons."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lemon"},{"term":"curry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of a family of dishes originating from Indian cuisine, flavored by a spiced sauce.","A spiced sauce or relish, especially one flavored with curry powder."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cook or season with curry powder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/curry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Monty Python","blocks":[{"text":"Monty Python, also known as the Pythons, were a British comedy troupe formed in 1969 consisting of Graham Chapman, John Cleese, Terry Gilliam, Eric Idle, Terry Jones and Michael Palin. The group initially came to prominence in the UK for the sketch comedy television series Monty Python's Flying Circus, which aired on the BBC from 1969 to 1974. Their work then developed into a larger collection that included live shows, films, albums, books, and musicals; their influence on comedy has been compared to the Beatles' influence on music. Their sketch show has been called \"an important moment in the evolution of television comedy\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/All_six_members_of_Monty_Python_in_1978.jpg/330px-All_six_members_of_Monty_Python_in_1978.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monty_Python","wikidata":"Q16402"},{"title":"Lemon","blocks":[{"text":"The lemon is a species of small evergreen tree in the Citrus genus of the flowering plant family Rutaceae. A true lemon is a hybrid of the citron and the bitter orange. Its origins are uncertain, but some evidence suggests lemons originated during the 1st millennium BC in what is now northeastern India. Some other citrus fruits are called lemon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/P1030323.JPG/330px-P1030323.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemon","wikidata":"Q500"},{"title":"Curry","blocks":[{"text":"Curry is a dish with a spicy sauce, initially in Indian cuisine, then modified by interchange with the Portuguese, followed by the British, and eventually thoroughly internationalised. Many curries are found in the cuisines of countries in Southeast Asia and East Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Taj_Mahal_-_Lamb_Curry_Madras.jpg/330px-Taj_Mahal_-_Lamb_Curry_Madras.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry","wikidata":"Q164606"},{"title":"Lemon (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lemon or lemon-color is a vivid yellow color characteristic of the lemon fruit. Shades of \"lemon\" may vary significantly from the fruit's actual color, including fluorescent tones and creamy hues reflective of lemon pies and confections."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Lemon_-_whole_and_split.jpg/330px-Lemon_-_whole_and_split.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemon_(color)","wikidata":"Q2720565"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/The_Four_Yorkshiremen%2C_2014_%28crop%29.jpg/500px-The_Four_Yorkshiremen%2C_2014_%28crop%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/The_Four_Yorkshiremen%2C_2014_%28crop%29.jpg/1280px-The_Four_Yorkshiremen%2C_2014_%28crop%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/The_Four_Yorkshiremen%2C_2014_%28crop%29.jpg/500px-The_Four_Yorkshiremen%2C_2014_%28crop%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The \"Four Yorkshiremen\" sketch at the 2014 Monty Python reunion. Written by Cleese, Chapman, Tim Brooke-Taylor and Marty Feldman, it was originally performed on their TV series At Last the 1948 Show in 1967. It parodies nostalgic conversations about humble beginnings or difficult childhoods.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Four_Yorkshiremen%2C_2014_(crop).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Outside_the_Television_Centre_%28geograph_5766769%29.jpg/500px-Outside_the_Television_Centre_%28geograph_5766769%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Outside_the_Television_Centre_%28geograph_5766769%29.jpg/1280px-Outside_the_Television_Centre_%28geograph_5766769%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Outside_the_Television_Centre_%28geograph_5766769%29.jpg/500px-Outside_the_Television_Centre_%28geograph_5766769%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Monty Python's Flying Circus was recorded at BBC Television Centre in west London (pictured) and on location around the UK. It debuted on the BBC on 5 October 1969.[22][23]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOutside_the_Television_Centre_(geograph_5766769).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Monty_Python_Live_02-07-14_12_46_43_%2814415411808%29.jpg/500px-Monty_Python_Live_02-07-14_12_46_43_%2814415411808%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi